-- Original, house-styled rework of ASTRO-101 Lecture 2. The Sun that moves among
-- the stars (the ECLIPTIC = the shadow of Earth's orbit; the zodiac; ~1 deg/day);
-- the OBLIQUITY (23.4 deg tilt of ecliptic to equator) and the four stations
-- (SOLSTICES and EQUINOXES); the great MISCONCEPTION demolished (seasons are NOT
-- distance -- Earth closest in January, ~3% variation, opposite hemispheres); the
-- true cause -- the TILT via two mechanisms (DIRECTNESS, sunlight angle ~ sin
-- altitude; and DURATION, day length); the GNOMON reading the season and the tilt
-- from a shadow; the tilt carving the TROPICS (+/-23.4) and POLAR CIRCLES
-- (+/-66.5); and two subtleties (THERMAL LAG and the ANALEMMA). Carries the course
-- through-line: every apparent rhythm is the shadow of a real geometry.
--
-- VISUALS (engagement pass): embeds a ```seasonmaker explorer (the Sun's daily
-- altitude curve -- peak = directness, width = duration -- with NO distance input;
-- SeasonMaker.tsx) and a ```gnomon explorer (a shadow-stick reading the season and
-- extracting the 23.4 deg tilt from two solstice shadows; Gnomon.tsx), three
-- interactive ```example practice boxes (noon-sun, season-intensity,
-- tilt-geography; WorkedExample.tsx), and {image: ...} markers resolving to real
-- imagery at read time. Body Postgres dollar-quoted (no literal currency and no
-- inline KaTeX, so a zero/even '$' count is expected).

